# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the telephone 1876, Marconi's radio across the Atlantic
# 1901, television 1936, the first computer 1945, Sputnik 1957, the web opened to all 1991. The
# message habits in the three-star question are made up.
lesson history-a-connected-world v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=5 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A world connected"
  goal "Put the ways of reaching people far away in order, name the ten years each came in, and say what each changed."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1860 to=2000 step=20 years=[1876, 1901, 1936, 1957, 1991] labels=["Telephone", "Radio across the Atlantic", "Television", "First satellite", "World Wide Web"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      In 1850 news crossed an ocean only as fast as a ship. Then one invention after another joined
      the world: the telephone in 1876, a radio signal across the Atlantic in 1901, television in
      1936. In 1957 the first satellite circled the Earth, and satellites soon carried calls and
      pictures round its curve. In 1991 the World Wide Web opened to everyone, and a message could
      reach the other side of the world in a moment.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.connect-order k=0
    }
    show history.connect-order k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.message-could k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.connect-decade k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.invention-between k=4 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show history.connect-order k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.message-could k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.connect-decade k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.what-replaced k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.message-could k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-satellites k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.messages-a-year k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "The telephone, radio, television, satellites and the web joined the world, each one faster and further than the last."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is how the world became connected in about a hundred years, from messages carried by
    ship to messages sent in a moment, and what that changed for families: news, jobs, friends far
    away. The way in orders three inventions; the core asks what could already be used in a given
    year, names decades and counts the years between inventions. The wrong idea to watch for is
    that the web and the telephone are nearly as old as each other; the line shows more than a
    hundred years between them. If the child is stuck, ask the oldest person you know how they
    kept in touch with someone far away when they were young. Off the page, trace how a message
    you send today travels. Japan's 社会 for grade five includes the role of information in
    people's lives (the course of study as read in its 2008 edition lists 情報 among the grade's
    topics); England's key stage 2 allows a theme "beyond 1066".
    Russia's history programme reaches the twentieth century's inventions in grade nine (recalled,
    not quoted here).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example orders the telephone, television and the web, the years asked about are far from each invention, and the decades are of the web and the satellite."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the inventions are close together, the years asked about are just after an invention came, the decades include 1901 and 1945, and the three-star family writes monthly and emails twice a week."
  }
}
